 {'problem': 'Self-hosted model weights loaded without a signature or format check', 'owner': 'C05'}]\n  A07.1 A model inventory in code or config lists every helper model the agent calls (verifiers, judges, rerankers, embedding models, world models). \n     1) Search for every model call: provider SDK clients, model name strings, embedding, rerank, moderation, and judge calls. 2) Compare that list with the model inventory. 3) Answer no if any helper model called in code is missing from the inventory, or no inventory exists.\n  A07.2 Each helper model is identified by a pinned version or digest plus its prompt and configuration, not by an alias. \n     1) For each helper model, find its model ID and follow env vars to the final value. 2) Check it is a pinned version or digest, and that its prompt and configuration are also recorded. 3) Answer no if any is an alias (`-latest`, undated name), falls back to one by default, or is hard-coded inline outside the inventory.\n  A07.3 Each helper model's decisions are logged with that model's identity. \n     1) Open each helper or checker call site. 2) Check its decision is logged with that model's ID and version. 3) Answer no if decisions are logged without model identity, or logged only on the allow or success branch.\n  A07.4 A checker timeout, error, invalid output, or version mismatch makes the control that depends on it fail (block or escalate), not pass by default. \n     1) Open each checker call site (judge, guardrail, moderation, verifier). 2) Read every `try/except`, `catch`, timeout, and parse-error branch. 3) Check each one blocks or escalates. 4) Answer no if any branch returns allow, skips the check, treats unparseable output as safe, or logs and continues. Also no if the returned model version is never compared with the expected one.\nC01 Complete release manifest G2 never\nWrite a release manifest: a list of everything that makes up this release. Include the container, harness, system prompt, rules files, model, built-in tools, MCP servers, permission scopes, memory and retrieval settings, identity bindings, network rules, and execution budgets. Include security-policy versions, MCP protocol and extension versions, approved tool-publication config, issuer/audience bindings, and cache/task isolation settings. Add a separate model record for every model: the main agent model, sub-agent models, and helper models such as embedding models and rerankers. For each, record the provider, model ID, exact version or checkpoint, and any adapter or fine-tune version. For training or fine-tuning you run yourself, also record the training job ID, base-model version, training code and config version, dataset version, and the digest of the output. The agent app's release number alone is not enough. Use the [model provenance fields](CHECKLIST-VERIFICATION.md#model-provenance-fields) to separate recorded versions from details a provider doesn't share. Check each model ID you request against the provider's versioning docs. Mark it as a pinned snapshot or as an alias that can change, and record which doc you used and its date. Naming rules differ by provider and model generation. Name each artifact's owner and a reference that can't change, where one exists. A machine-readable format, such as CycloneDX ML-BOM or the SPDX 3.0 AI and Dataset profiles, lets tools check the manifest.\n[{'problem': 'Running setup not checked against the approved manifest', 'owner': 'C03'}, {'problem': 'Manifest changes made without a reviewed diff and named author', 'owner': 'C02'}, {'problem': 'Running config drifting from the approved baseline unnoticed', 'owner': 'C04'}, {'problem': 'Agent-type-id not computed as a hash of its identity inputs', 'owner': 'A03'}]\n  C01.1 A release manifest file lists the container, harness, system prompt, rules files, model, built-in tools, MCP servers, permission scopes, memory and retrieval settings, identity bindings, network rules, execution budgets, and security-policy versions. \n     1) Find the release manifest (`.brace/manifest.yaml`, release manifest, ML-BOM). 2) Check each category: container, harness, system prompt, rules files, model, built-in tools, MCP servers, permission scopes, memory and retrieval, identity bindings, network rules, budgets, security-policy versions. 3) Compare with what config sets up (`*.mcp.json`, NetworkPolicy). 4) Answer no if any category is missing or configured items are left out.\n  C01.2 The manifest has a separate model record for every model (main, sub-agent, and helper models such as embedding models and rerankers), each with provider, model ID, exact version or checkpoint, and adapter or fine-tune version. \n     1) Search code for every model used: main, sub-agent, embedding, reranker, judge. 2) Check the manifest has a separate record for each. 3) Check each record has provider, model ID, exact version or checkpoint, and adapter or fine-tune version. 4) Answer no if any model in code has no record, or a record is missing a field.\n  C01.3 Each requested model ID is labeled as a pinned snapshot or a changeable alias, with the provider doc and date used. \n     1) Open each model record in the manifest. 2) Check it has a pinned-or-alias label, the provider doc used, and a date. 3) Answer no if any record lacks one of these, or labels an undated or `-latest` name as pinned.\n  C01.4 Each artifact has a named owner and a reference that can't change (digest or commit) where one exists, not a mutable tag or the app's release number. \n     1) For each manifest artifact, check for a named owner. 2) Check its reference is a digest (`sha256:`) or commit SHA where one exists. 3) Answer no if any artifact has no owner, or uses a mutable tag (`:latest`, branch name) or the app release number where a digest or commit was available.\n  C01.5 For training the team runs itself, the manifest records the training job ID, base-model version, training code and config version, dataset version, and output digest. \n     1) Search for training or fine-tuning code (training jobs, `Trainer`, fine-tune API calls, pipeline configs). 2) If found, check the manifest records job ID, base-model version, training code and config version, dataset version, and output digest. 3) Answer no if training runs here and any field is missing. Answer unknown if training may run in another repo.\nC02 Review security-relevant changes G2 never\nRequire diffs with a named author, plus review, for changes to the manifest and its policies. Show who is allowed to change production config. Record emergency changes with an owner, a reason, an end date, and a follow-up review.\n[{'problem': 'Running config drifting from the approved baseline unnoticed', 'owner': 'C04'}, {'problem': 'Release candidate not re-tested against attack cases', 'owner': 'C06'}, {'problem': 'No tested rollback to an approved setup', 'owner': 'C07'}]\n  C02.1 The manifest, prompts, tool config, and policy files live in version control, and merges require review by someone other than the named author. \n     1) Check the manifest, prompts, tool config, and policy files are tracked in git, not loaded at runtime from an untracked path or remote URL. 2) Look for branch protection or ruleset config requiring approving review, and CODEOWNERS covering those paths. 3) Answer no if any of these files is untracked or not covered. Answer unknown if review rules are set only in the hosting service.\n  C02.2 Only named roles in config (CODEOWNERS, IAM, or protected CI environments) can change production configuration. \n     1) Open CODEOWNERS, IAM policies, and CI environment config (protected `environment:` entries). 2) Check production config paths and deploy jobs are limited to named roles. 3) Answer no if a catch-all `*` owner, a broad group, or the agent's own credentials can change production config, or a deploy job skips the protected environment.\n  C02.3 An emergency change path exists that records an owner, a reason, an end date, and a follow-up review. \n     1) Search for an emergency or break-glass path (runbook, workflow, CLI). 2) Check it records owner, reason, end date, and follow-up review. 3) Answer no if any of the four is missing, or the path bypasses review with no record. Answer unknown if the procedure lives outside this repo.\n./migrations/001_init.sql\n./requirements.txt\n./config/agent.yaml\n./config/tools.yaml\n./Dockerfile\n./k8s/deployment.yaml\n./k8s/audit-maintenance.yaml\n./k8s/admission.yaml\n./k8s/networkpolicy.yaml\n./CODEOWNERS\n./.mcp.json\n./release/manifest.yaml\n./README.md\n./.github/workflows/release.yml\n./prompts/system.md\n./src/killswitch.py\n./src/gateway_client.py\n./src/gateway.py\n./src/audit.py\n./src/approvals.py\n./src/credentials.py\n./src/tools.py\n./src/type_id.py\n./src/redact.py\n./src/retrieval.py\n./src/agent.py\n./src/budgets.py\n./src/main.py\n./src/validation.py\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File \"/private/var/folders/6v/yk9bzvrn5zx0kxll7d4szzb80000gn/T/brace-batch-6qhe_9tj/agent/../skill/scripts/review_coverage.py\", line 17, in <module>\n    from prescan import walk, MAX_FILE_BYTES, COMMENT_LINE\nModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'prescan'","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01AiSkxxYqFWKiAhKcCdchnZ","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
